Native SwiftUI parity of the web `CronParserTool` — a ToolCard-framed cron
expression parser. Seven files, mirroring the sibling devtool surfaces
(UrlEncoder/Base64Tool):

  CronParser.Adapter.swift   Pure engine: faithful port of web describeCron /
                             getNextCronRuns / matchField + the field tokenizer,
                             CronParserResult projection, preset table, a11y
                             summary. Foundation-only, deterministic (injected
                             now + Calendar). Reproduces the web quirks exactly
                             (step-over-range, JS Number range bounds, weekday
                             name fallback, padStart, parseInt semantics).
  CronParser.Model.swift     @MainActor @Observable CronParserModel (P1/S8 seam),
                             CronParserTelemetry seam (P1/S11 view.opened),
                             CronParserStrings i18n facade (P1/S10).
  CronParser.swift           CronParserView surface (ToolCard chrome + states).
  CronParser.Views.swift     Header, expression field (leading timer glyph),
                             wrapping preset chips (custom flow Layout),
                             description panel, upcoming-runs list, empty hint.
  CronParser.Previews.swift  5 previews (empty / every-5-min / daily / weekly /
                             no-upcoming-runs) on a fixed clock.
  CronParser.Tests.swift     Engine parity + model derivation + preset apply +
                             telemetry-once + a11y summary + i18n facade tests.
  CronParser.strings         P1/S10 catalog contribution (10 parity keys + 4
                             native-chrome keys).

States rendered (every one — never a blank box): empty hint (web hides output),
parsed description, upcoming-runs list, and a friendly "no upcoming runs" note
for impossible schedules (web hides the list). The web source has NO data hooks
beyond useTranslation (pure client-side parser), so the network states
loading/error/stale/offline do not apply — documented in-source, same
disposition as the sibling UrlEncoder/Base64Tool surfaces.

